FigJam — Research & Design Process: 
https://www.figma.com/board/BsjdWprX6uCknj1HMH19ec/HuskeyFInd-Draft?node-id=0-1&t=hBXoklOn0kpzp6Oa-1

The full end-to-end process board documenting how HuskyFind went from a research question to a tested design. 
It includes the original empathy map built from one-on-one interviews with UW students about losing or finding 
items on campus (the direct quotes, the frustration and resignation that came with it, and the workaround behaviors
like posting in Discord servers or just giving up and paying to replace the item); two personas — Maya Chen, a UW 
student who regularly misplaces items between classes, and David Ramirez, front-desk staff managing lost items with 
no shared system — built to keep design decisions grounded in real stakeholder needs; the "How might we" problem 
statement that shaped the whole project; a competitive review of existing options (building front desks, UW R
eddit/Facebook groups, generic lost & found apps) and why none of them solve the problem; low-fidelity wireframes 
and the ideation/prioritization work (MoSCoW-style: must-have, should-have, nice-to-have) that decided which features 
made the cut; and the two rounds of usability testing (Wizard of Oz and think-aloud) with the specific before/after 
design changes each one produced — adding a "Message Finder" button and claimed-item status after testing showed users
couldn't tell how to contact a finder, and adding an AI privacy scanner and a confirmation screen after testing showed
users were unsure how to safely post sensitive items.
